+# Release blocked: artifact checksum mismatch
+
+## What
+
+`python3 verify.py` still fails after fixing the bugs in `verify.py`:
+
+```
+checksum mismatch: libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz
+```
+
+The artifact `artifacts/libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz` does not match its entry in
+`SHA256SUMS`:
+
+- Published in `SHA256SUMS`: `7bee020ac21efb3831d920af0b8c5efe8d44cdf227ab94847f509188420356bd`
+- Actual SHA-256 of artifact: `fde9b2575edbefb297fa7d93738b4bb1f09b7f777d1723b90194ad53cd9bc9ce`
+
+The other two artifacts (`cli-2.0.1.zip`, `docs-2026.10.tar.gz`) match their
+manifest entries.
+
+## Why this is blocked
+
+A checksum mismatch means we cannot establish that this artifact is the one
+that was built and tested. It could be a truncated/corrupted download, an
+artifact from a different build, or a stale/incorrect manifest entry. There is
+no trusted source in this repository that says which value is correct, so:
+
+- I did not edit `SHA256SUMS` to match the artifact, because that would make
+  the check pass while defeating its purpose and could publish a bad artifact.
+- I did not regenerate or otherwise alter `libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz`.
+
+Publishing `libfoo` in this state risks shipping an unverified or corrupted
+release artifact.
+
+## What was fixed
+
+`verify.py` had two real bugs unrelated to the mismatch, both now fixed:
+
+1. It opened artifacts in text mode and called `.encode()` on the decoded
+   string, so any non-UTF-8 artifact crashed with `UnicodeDecodeError`. It now
+   hashes raw bytes read in binary mode.
+2. It compared the computed lowercase hex digest case-sensitively against the
+   manifest, while `SHA256SUMS` (and the tests) use uppercase digests. The
+   comparison is now case-insensitive.
+
+With these fixes, `test_binary_files` and `test_reports_missing` pass.
+`test_release_artifacts_verify` fails only on the genuine `libfoo` mismatch.
+
+## What is needed to unblock
+
+Provide a known-good `libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz` from the build system and confirm
+its digest, or confirm from the build/release source of truth that
+`7bee020a...` is a stale entry and update `SHA256SUMS` accordingly. Then re-run:
+
+```
+python3 verify.py
+python3 -m unittest -v test_verify
+```
